    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Apache speak languages of the Athabaskan family, related to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a>, and came into the Southwest from the north several centuries before the Spanish arrived.</li><li>Each Apache tribe is politically autonomous; historically the six major groups were independent of one another and sometimes fought each other.</li><li>After centuries of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and forced removals onto reservations, the Apache population has rebounded to 148,936 in the 2020 census.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the reservations, Apache families were mostly matrilocal: a married daughter's new dwelling was built near her mother's, and her husband joined her family, while among the Chiricahua a man avoided even the sight of his wife's female relatives.</p></aside>
    </section>

